1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:00,591
Same well.

2
00:00:00,691 --> 00:00:01,617
Same production.

3
00:00:01,717 --> 00:00:02,258
Same heat.

4
00:00:02,358 --> 00:00:05,912
The only difference is one boundary condition at the top.

5
00:00:06,012 --> 00:00:09,053
On the left, the wellhead is free to grow upward.

6
00:00:09,153 --> 00:00:13,861
On the right, the wellhead and the conductor are held, so they cannot move.

7
00:00:14,383 --> 00:00:16,738
Let's compare the two cases side by side.

8
00:00:16,838 --> 00:00:21,167
The heat up, the forces on the wellhead, the conductor and surface casing,

9
00:00:21,267 --> 00:00:23,382
the inner casings and their buckling.

10
00:00:23,482 --> 00:00:26,495
And let's find out where the difference really lies.

11
00:00:27,817 --> 00:00:28,856
Production starts.

12
00:00:28,956 --> 00:00:33,982
Oil at about one hundred and eight degrees warms the casings from the inside out,

13
00:00:34,082 --> 00:00:36,324
and every string wants to get longer.

14
00:00:36,424 --> 00:00:40,944
The free sections of the inner casings want to grow by one to two metres.

15
00:00:41,483 --> 00:00:43,465
On the left, the wellhead rises.

16
00:00:43,565 --> 00:00:48,602
After one day it has grown about thirty millimetres, after a month about fifty,

17
00:00:48,702 --> 00:00:53,805
and after a year about sixty one, as the conductor and the surface casing slowly

18
00:00:53,905 --> 00:00:54,326
warm up.

19
00:00:54,850 --> 00:00:56,540
On the right, nothing moves.

20
00:00:56,640 --> 00:00:59,288
Instead, the force on the restraint climbs.

21
00:00:59,388 --> 00:01:03,251
About twelve meganewtons after a day, seventeen after a month,

22
00:01:03,351 --> 00:01:05,616
and nineteen and a half after a year.

23
00:01:05,716 --> 00:01:09,963
The same thermal expansion, showing up as force instead of movement.

24
00:01:11,883 --> 00:01:13,253
The principle is simple.

25
00:01:13,353 --> 00:01:17,600
A heated steel member that is free to move gets longer, with no stress.

26
00:01:17,700 --> 00:01:20,540
A member that cannot move stays the same length,

27
00:01:20,640 --> 00:01:25,254
and instead carries about two and a half megapascals of compression for every

28
00:01:25,354 --> 00:01:25,683
degree.

29
00:01:25,783 --> 00:01:28,745
Movement and force are two sides of the same coin.

30
00:01:29,983 --> 00:01:33,068
The well is a set of springs tied to one wellhead.

31
00:01:33,168 --> 00:01:35,998
The long, free inner casings are soft springs.

32
00:01:36,098 --> 00:01:40,712
The conductor and the cemented surface casing, gripped by cement and soil,

33
00:01:40,812 --> 00:01:44,661
form one very stiff spring, more than a hundred times stiffer.

34
00:01:45,183 --> 00:01:47,992
That stiffness ratio decides almost everything.

35
00:01:48,092 --> 00:01:50,158
Because the foundation is so stiff,

36
00:01:50,258 --> 00:01:54,738
the inner strings cannot push the wellhead very far, even when it is free.

37
00:01:54,838 --> 00:01:59,751
They are already held back, and carry ninety to ninety six percent of their fully

38
00:01:59,851 --> 00:02:00,742
restrained load.

39
00:02:02,683 --> 00:02:05,777
Now look at the forces acting on the wellhead itself.

40
00:02:05,877 --> 00:02:10,417
With a free wellhead, the hot inner strings push up with about four point one

41
00:02:10,517 --> 00:02:11,140
meganewtons.

42
00:02:11,240 --> 00:02:16,021
The wellhead rises until the conductor and surface casing are stretched enough to

43
00:02:16,121 --> 00:02:18,371
pull back with the same four point one.

44
00:02:18,471 --> 00:02:19,696
The net force is zero.

45
00:02:20,217 --> 00:02:24,410
With the wellhead restrained, the inner strings push up slightly harder,

46
00:02:24,510 --> 00:02:26,140
four point three meganewtons.

47
00:02:26,240 --> 00:02:29,778
But now the conductor and the surface casing push up as well,

48
00:02:29,878 --> 00:02:34,310
with about fifteen meganewtons, because their own expansion at the seabed is

49
00:02:34,410 --> 00:02:34,787
blocked.

50
00:02:34,887 --> 00:02:36,874
The restraint has to react the sum.

51
00:02:36,974 --> 00:02:41,347
Nineteen and a half meganewtons, roughly the weight of two thousand tonnes.

52
00:02:42,683 --> 00:02:45,156
This is where the two cases really differ.

53
00:02:45,256 --> 00:02:49,260
Here is the axial force along the conductor and the surface casing,

54
00:02:49,360 --> 00:02:51,097
in the top two hundred metres.

55
00:02:51,197 --> 00:02:53,914
Tension to the right, compression to the left.

56
00:02:54,450 --> 00:02:54,672
Free.

57
00:02:54,772 --> 00:02:58,471
At the seabed, the inner strings pull the composite upward,

58
00:02:58,571 --> 00:03:00,081
so the top is in tension.

59
00:03:00,181 --> 00:03:03,430
A few tens of metres down, soil friction takes over,

60
00:03:03,530 --> 00:03:07,422
and the casing's own thermal expansion turns into compression.

61
00:03:07,950 --> 00:03:08,517
Restrained.

62
00:03:08,617 --> 00:03:13,369
The top is locked, so the full thermal compression reaches all the way up to the

63
00:03:13,469 --> 00:03:13,915
wellhead.

64
00:03:14,015 --> 00:03:16,341
About fifteen meganewtons at the seabed.

65
00:03:16,883 --> 00:03:20,518
Below about a hundred metres, the two curves are identical.

66
00:03:20,618 --> 00:03:25,201
Cement and rock already hold the surface casing completely, in both cases.

67
00:03:25,301 --> 00:03:28,493
The whole difference sits in the top hundred metres,

68
00:03:28,593 --> 00:03:31,341
where the soft soil lets the conductor slide.

69
00:03:31,883 --> 00:03:36,384
In the steel, that is a change of about one hundred and ninety megapascals at the

70
00:03:36,484 --> 00:03:36,782
seabed.

71
00:03:36,882 --> 00:03:41,042
From plus forty four, to minus one hundred and forty four in the conductor,

72
00:03:41,142 --> 00:03:45,131
and from plus eight, to minus one hundred and eighty in the twenty inch.

73
00:03:45,231 --> 00:03:49,164
Just below the conductor shoe, where the conductor hands its load over,

74
00:03:49,264 --> 00:03:53,538
the twenty inch locally reaches about three hundred megapascals in our model.

75
00:03:53,638 --> 00:03:55,299
Around eighty percent of yield.

76
00:03:56,617 --> 00:03:57,827
Now the inner casings.

77
00:03:57,927 --> 00:04:02,591
The thirteen and three eighths picks up two point two meganewtons of compression

78
00:04:02,691 --> 00:04:06,045
with a free wellhead, and two point three when restrained.

79
00:04:06,145 --> 00:04:07,415
Only five percent more.

80
00:04:07,950 --> 00:04:13,085
In both cases its effective force is compressive along the entire thirteen hundred

81
00:04:13,185 --> 00:04:14,234
metre free length.

82
00:04:14,334 --> 00:04:18,639
So it is helically buckled in both cases, with almost the same pitch,

83
00:04:18,739 --> 00:04:20,746
bending stress and contact force.

84
00:04:21,283 --> 00:04:25,811
For the nine and five eighths, the buckled zone starts at six hundred and ninety

85
00:04:25,911 --> 00:04:30,207
metres with a free wellhead, and at five hundred and ninety four metres when

86
00:04:30,307 --> 00:04:30,844
restrained.

87
00:04:30,944 --> 00:04:35,298
About a hundred metres more buckled pipe, for three percent more compression.

88
00:04:35,817 --> 00:04:36,535
Why so small?

89
00:04:36,635 --> 00:04:40,122
When free, the wellhead moves only sixty one millimetres.

90
00:04:40,222 --> 00:04:44,339
Compared with the one to two metres the inner strings want to grow,

91
00:04:44,439 --> 00:04:45,912
that is almost no relief.

92
00:04:46,012 --> 00:04:49,752
Restraining the wellhead takes away a relief they hardly had.

93
00:04:51,083 --> 00:04:52,745
Here is the full comparison.

94
00:04:53,283 --> 00:04:56,014
For the inner casings, the numbers barely move.

95
00:04:56,114 --> 00:04:58,423
Compression rises three to five percent,

96
00:04:58,523 --> 00:05:03,422
the highest combined stress in the thirteen and three eighths goes from two hundred

97
00:05:03,522 --> 00:05:07,939
and seventeen to two hundred and twenty nine megapascals, well below yield,

98
00:05:08,039 --> 00:05:12,034
and the uplift on its hanger from zero point nine to one meganewton.

99
00:05:12,583 --> 00:05:15,681
The rows that change dramatically are at the bottom.

100
00:05:15,781 --> 00:05:19,801
The conductor and surface casing stresses, and the restraint force,

101
00:05:19,901 --> 00:05:23,245
which goes from zero to nineteen and a half meganewtons.

102
00:05:25,583 --> 00:05:28,216
In practice, no restraint is perfectly rigid.

103
00:05:28,316 --> 00:05:32,468
Any real restraint lands somewhere on this line between the two cases.

104
00:05:32,568 --> 00:05:37,023
Halving the growth to twenty eight millimetres still costs eight and a half

105
00:05:37,123 --> 00:05:37,752
meganewtons.

106
00:05:37,852 --> 00:05:41,821
Roughly a third of a meganewton for every millimetre you take away.

107
00:05:42,350 --> 00:05:43,942
And the load comes and goes.

108
00:05:44,042 --> 00:05:47,810
Every shut in cools the well, and every start up heats it again.

109
00:05:47,910 --> 00:05:50,288
A free wellhead simply moves up and down.

110
00:05:50,388 --> 00:05:53,733
A restrained one cycles its full force, on the restraint,

111
00:05:53,833 --> 00:05:57,661
the conductor and the wellhead housing, for the life of the well.

112
00:05:59,583 --> 00:06:03,965
There is one situation where the comparison changes for a casing string.

113
00:06:04,065 --> 00:06:07,202
A surface casing that is not cemented to the seabed.

114
00:06:07,302 --> 00:06:11,310
Then the twenty inch has its own free length inside the conductor,

115
00:06:11,410 --> 00:06:13,426
and the foundation is much softer.

116
00:06:13,950 --> 00:06:16,604
Free, the wellhead grows seventy millimetres,

117
00:06:16,704 --> 00:06:19,970
and the twenty inch carries two and a half meganewtons.

118
00:06:20,070 --> 00:06:22,480
Restrained, that jumps to four point two.

119
00:06:22,580 --> 00:06:26,703
Sixty eight percent more, and it buckles harder inside the conductor.

120
00:06:26,803 --> 00:06:28,845
Here, the restraint really matters.

121
00:06:30,783 --> 00:06:32,580
So what is the real difference?

122
00:06:32,680 --> 00:06:34,721
For the inner casings, very little.

123
00:06:34,821 --> 00:06:36,923
They buckle helically in both cases,

124
00:06:37,023 --> 00:06:39,982
and the restraint adds only three to five percent.

125
00:06:40,517 --> 00:06:42,738
For the conductor, the surface casing,

126
00:06:42,838 --> 00:06:45,548
the wellhead housing and the restraint itself,

127
00:06:45,648 --> 00:06:49,764
it is the difference between almost no load, and tens of meganewtons,

128
00:06:49,864 --> 00:06:51,474
cycling with every start up.

129
00:06:52,017 --> 00:06:55,448
Free or restrained, the thermal expansion is the same.

130
00:06:55,548 --> 00:06:59,634
The only choice is whether it shows up as movement, or as force.
